-\subsection{How AutoPareto works}\label{sec:brief}
-\looseness=-1 \textbf{The human sets the rules.} The task is to train a small Transformer language model on web text (ClimbMix) for 300\,s on one NVIDIA A40, starting from 8 layers, width 512 and 50.3M parameters. Each candidate is scored on validation BPB, decode throughput and peak inference memory. The agent may edit only \texttt{train.py}, which holds the model, optimizer and training loop but no generation code. A documented hook lets the agent add its own generation code, which the protected benchmark then times (Appendix~\ref{app:access}).
+\looseness=-1 \textbf{Setup.} Each candidate trains a small Transformer on web text (ClimbMix) for 300\,s on one NVIDIA A40, starting from 8 layers and 50.3M parameters. The agent edits only \texttt{train.py} (model, optimizer, training loop); a documented hook lets it add generation code, which the protected benchmark times (Appendix~\ref{app:access}). Each \emph{attempt} reads all earlier ones, picks a \emph{parent}, records a hypothesis and edits the file; protected code trains, measures and records it, failures included (Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:loop}). A recipe is on the \emph{Pareto frontier} if no other is at least as good on every objective and better on one.
 
-\looseness=-1 \textbf{The agent runs the loop} (Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:loop}). In each \emph{attempt} it reads all earlier attempts, picks a \emph{parent} recipe, records a hypothesis and edits \texttt{train.py}; protected code trains, measures and records the result, failures included. A recipe is on the \emph{Pareto frontier} if no other is at least as good on all three objectives and better on one.
