-\subsection{Did the discoveries hold up?}\label{sec:verify}
-\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why equal tokens.} Under a time budget a faster recipe also trains on more data, so its score mixes learning per token with speed; equal tokens isolate learning per token. A final pre-registered test retrained all 13 recipes on new seeds 6 to 8 for exactly 201.3M tokens and for 300\,s, and timed all of them with one inference benchmark on one A40 (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}, Appendix Table~\ref{tab:verify}); AR-ref's, GPT A22's and Opus's matched-token runs reuse the earlier pre-registered retraining (E1), which drift controls reproduced.
+\subsection{Comparing all recipes fairly}\label{sec:verify}
+\looseness=-1 \textbf{One test for all 13 recipes.} To separate learning per token from speed, a final pre-registered test retrained all 13 recipes on new seeds 6 to 8 for 201.3M tokens and for 300\,s and timed each on one benchmark and A40 (Table~\ref{tab:verify}), reusing E1's and the Opus retraining's runs, checked by drift controls.
 
-\looseness=-1 \textbf{Equal tokens: AR-ref learns most per token.} All \CRSixTokWorseCount{} other recipes are worse after 201.3M tokens, by \CRSixTokDLo{} to \CRSixTokDHi{} held-out BPB, each 95\% interval above zero (paired by seed): GPT A22 by \CRSixGptTokD{} and Opus's recipes by about 0.06. \textbf{Equal time: not resolved.} At 300\,s no recipe is resolved as better than AR-ref: GPT A22 differs by \CRSixGptTimeD{} \CRSixGptTimeCI{} and Opus A27 by \CRSixOpTwSevenTimeD{} \CRSixOpTwSevenTimeCI, which is not evidence of equality, and \CRSixTimeResolved{} recipes are resolved as worse.
