-\subsection{Why Opus decodes about 32$\times$ faster but generates only about 5$\times$ faster}\label{sec:inference}
-\looseness=-1 After reading the prompt (\emph{prefill}), a model generates one token per \emph{decode} step, and only these are scored. At batch size one they are dominated by CPU launches of GPU programs (\emph{kernels}), \CRIAKernBase{} per token at first: a \emph{CUDA graph} replays recorded kernels with one call and \emph{kernel fusion} merges them (\CRIAKernEager{} become \CRIAKernFused).
+\looseness=-1 \textbf{No recipe wins everything} (Figures~\ref{fig:overview} and~\ref{fig:unifiedscatter}). At equal tokens \CRSixFrontEveryTok{} are on the frontier in every seed (the Opus recipes are \CRSixOpDecSpread\% apart in decode speed, so which of them is not resolved); at 300\,s the leaders' quality is not separated, so decode speed decides.
 
+\looseness=-1 \textbf{Downstream tasks} (Appendix Tables~\ref{tab:verify} and~\ref{tab:lmeval}). AR-ref also leads BLiMP, narrowly, and WikiText perplexity, an independent language-modelling check that ranks recipes like held-out BPB ($\rho=\CRSixRhoWiki$). Smaller recipes lead ARC-Easy, PIQA and LAMBADA by at most \CRSixLeadRunnerMax{} points over the runner-up, within the seed SDs (up to \CRSixAccSDMax), not a ranking, and five of seven further tasks are near chance at this scale.
+
+\looseness=-1 \textbf{Speed measurements.} Decode rates are medians over eight timed windows, each bracketed by baseline windows. \CRSixUnstableRuns{} of the 39 runs exceeded the benchmark's stability limits on these ratios, a timing flag, not a correctness failure. We report them as measured (rings in Figure~\ref{fig:unifiedscatter}); dropping them moves no mean by more than \CRSixStableShiftMax\% and leaves the frontier unchanged (Appendix~\ref{app:repro}). The 32$\times$ is far outside this noise; few-percent gaps between the slower recipes are not.
